{"ui_id":"u_000402_01","response_id":"r_000402_01","query_id":"q_000402","intent":"Technical support","tags":["networking","hardware setup","router"],"intent_bucket":"technical_support","genui_json":[{"version":"v0.9","createSurface":{"surfaceId":"surface_q_000402","catalogId":"https://genui.local/specification/v0_9/standard_catalog.json"}},{"version":"v0.9","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"surface_q_000402","components":[{"id":"root","component":"Column","children":["text_1"]},{"id":"text_1","component":"Text","text":"Assumptions:\n- You are currently connected to your Linksys EA8300 router, either via Wi-Fi or an Ethernet cable.\n- You know the current administrative password for your router. If you have never changed it, try the default passwords mentioned below. If you don't know it and the defaults don't work, you may need to perform a factory reset (which is beyond the scope of this guide and would erase all your custom settings).\n\nDefault Router Access Information for Linksys EA8300:\n- Local IP Address: 192.168.1.1\n- Standard Administrative Username: admin\n- Standard Administrative Password: admin or password (or sometimes it's blank). Always check the label on the bottom or back of your physical router for the most accurate default login details.\n\nStep-by-Step Guide to Change SSID and WPA2 Password:\n\n1.  Access Your Router's Web Interface:\n    - Open a web browser (Chrome, Firefox, Edge, Safari, etc.) on a device connected to your Linksys EA8300 router.\n    - In the address bar, type the default local IP address: 192.168.1.1 and press Enter.\n\n2.  Log In to Your Router:\n    - A login screen will appear.\n    - Enter the Username (typically \"admin\") and the Password (your current router administrative password).\n    - Click \"Log In\" or \"Sign In\".\n\n3.  Navigate to Wireless Settings:\n    - Once logged in, you will usually see a dashboard or main menu.\n    - Look for sections like \"Wi-Fi Settings\", \"Wireless\", \"Connectivity\", or \"Network\". For Linksys, it's often under \"Wi-Fi\" or \"Wireless\".\n\n4.  Change the Wi-Fi Network Name (SSID):\n    - Within the Wireless settings, you will likely see separate sections for your 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz bands. You can change them individually or simultaneously if your router offers a \"Smart Connect\" or \"Band Steering\" feature that merges them into one SSID.\n    - Locate the \"Network Name (SSID)\" field for both 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz bands.\n    - Delete the existing name and type in your desired new Wi-Fi network name. Choose a name that is easy for you to recognize.\n\n5.  Change the Wi-Fi Password (WPA2 Security Key):\n    - In the same Wireless settings section, find the \"Security Mode\" or \"Security Type\" option. Ensure it is set to \"WPA2 Personal\" or \"WPA2/WPA3 Personal\" for the best security.\n    - Locate the \"Password\", \"Network Key\", or \"Passphrase\" field.\n    - Delete the existing password and type in your new desired Wi-Fi password.\n    - Choose a strong password that is at least 8 characters long, combining uppercase and lowercase letters, numbers, and symbols.\n\n6.  Save Your Changes:\n    - After making your changes to both the SSID and password, look for a \"Apply\", \"Save\", or \"OK\" button, usually at the bottom of the page. Click it to save your new settings.\n\n7.  Reconnect Your Devices:\n    - Your router will likely restart or refresh its wireless network.\n    - All devices previously connected to your Wi-Fi will be disconnected.\n    - On each device (phone, laptop, tablet, smart home devices), you will need to forget the old Wi-Fi network and then search for your new Wi-Fi network name (SSID).\n    - Select your new SSID and enter the new Wi-Fi password you just created to reconnect.\n\nImportant Note: Always remember your new SSID and Wi-Fi password. It's a good idea to write them down and keep them in a secure place.\n\nQuick Actions:\n[Button: Linksys EA8300 Support Page] https://www.linksys.com/us/support/products/routers/EA8300.html\n\nSources:\n- https://www.linksys.com/us/support/products/routers/EA8300.html","variant":"body"}]}}],"assets":[],"toon":"[2]:\n  - version: v0.9\n    createSurface:\n      catalogId: \"https://genui.local/specification/v0_9/standard_catalog.json\"\n      surfaceId: surface_q_000402\n  - version: v0.9\n    updateComponents:\n      surfaceId: surface_q_000402\n      components[2]:\n        - component: Column\n          id: root\n          children[1]: text_1\n        - component: Text\n          id: text_1\n          text: \"Assumptions:\\n- You are currently connected to your Linksys EA8300 router, either via Wi-Fi or an Ethernet cable.\\n- You know the current administrative password for your router. If you have never changed it, try the default passwords mentioned below. If you don't know it and the defaults don't work, you may need to perform a factory reset (which is beyond the scope of this guide and would erase all your custom settings).\\n\\nDefault Router Access Information for Linksys EA8300:\\n- Local IP Address: 192.168.1.1\\n- Standard Administrative Username: admin\\n- Standard Administrative Password: admin or password (or sometimes it's blank). Always check the label on the bottom or back of your physical router for the most accurate default login details.\\n\\nStep-by-Step Guide to Change SSID and WPA2 Password:\\n\\n1.  Access Your Router's Web Interface:\\n    - Open a web browser (Chrome, Firefox, Edge, Safari, etc.) on a device connected to your Linksys EA8300 router.\\n    - In the address bar, type the default local IP address: 192.168.1.1 and press Enter.\\n\\n2.  Log In to Your Router:\\n    - A login screen will appear.\\n    - Enter the Username (typically \\\"admin\\\") and the Password (your current router administrative password).\\n    - Click \\\"Log In\\\" or \\\"Sign In\\\".\\n\\n3.  Navigate to Wireless Settings:\\n    - Once logged in, you will usually see a dashboard or main menu.\\n    - Look for sections like \\\"Wi-Fi Settings\\\", \\\"Wireless\\\", \\\"Connectivity\\\", or \\\"Network\\\". For Linksys, it's often under \\\"Wi-Fi\\\" or \\\"Wireless\\\".\\n\\n4.  Change the Wi-Fi Network Name (SSID):\\n    - Within the Wireless settings, you will likely see separate sections for your 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz bands. You can change them individually or simultaneously if your router offers a \\\"Smart Connect\\\" or \\\"Band Steering\\\" feature that merges them into one SSID.\\n    - Locate the \\\"Network Name (SSID)\\\" field for both 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz bands.\\n    - Delete the existing name and type in your desired new Wi-Fi network name. Choose a name that is easy for you to recognize.\\n\\n5.  Change the Wi-Fi Password (WPA2 Security Key):\\n    - In the same Wireless settings section, find the \\\"Security Mode\\\" or \\\"Security Type\\\" option. Ensure it is set to \\\"WPA2 Personal\\\" or \\\"WPA2/WPA3 Personal\\\" for the best security.\\n    - Locate the \\\"Password\\\", \\\"Network Key\\\", or \\\"Passphrase\\\" field.\\n    - Delete the existing password and type in your new desired Wi-Fi password.\\n    - Choose a strong password that is at least 8 characters long, combining uppercase and lowercase letters, numbers, and symbols.\\n\\n6.  Save Your Changes:\\n    - After making your changes to both the SSID and password, look for a \\\"Apply\\\", \\\"Save\\\", or \\\"OK\\\" button, usually at the bottom of the page. Click it to save your new settings.\\n\\n7.  Reconnect Your Devices:\\n    - Your router will likely restart or refresh its wireless network.\\n    - All devices previously connected to your Wi-Fi will be disconnected.\\n    - On each device (phone, laptop, tablet, smart home devices), you will need to forget the old Wi-Fi network and then search for your new Wi-Fi network name (SSID).\\n    - Select your new SSID and enter the new Wi-Fi password you just created to reconnect.\\n\\nImportant Note: Always remember your new SSID and Wi-Fi password. It's a good idea to write them down and keep them in a secure place.\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Linksys EA8300 Support Page] https://www.linksys.com/us/support/products/routers/EA8300.html\\n\\nSources:\\n- https://www.linksys.com/us/support/products/routers/EA8300.html\"\n          variant: body","validation":{"json_parse_ok":true,"schema_valid_strict":true,"schema_valid_lenient":true,"toon_roundtrip_ok":true,"errors":["fallback_generated"],"repair_attempts":0,"repair_needed":false},"metrics":{"content_coverage":1.0,"dup_rate":0.025,"lint_score":1.0,"output_tokens_toon":3270,"output_tokens_json":3335,"output_chars_toon":3270,"output_chars_json":3335,"component_count":2.0,"component_count_norm":0.03333333333333333,"component_count_capped":2.0,"unique_component_types":2.0,"max_tree_depth":2.0,"avg_tree_depth":1.5,"container_to_text_ratio":1.0,"information_chunking_score":0.0,"ui_modularity_score":0.0,"ui_decomposition_score":0.2833333333333333,"actionable_elements":0.0,"action_coverage":0.0,"url_as_text_rate":1.0,"table_pattern_detected":0.0,"table_cell_coverage":0.0,"section_heading_coverage":0.0,"markdown_leakage_rate":0.49019607843137253,"missing_ids":0.0,"missing_ids_rate":0.0,"dangling_components":0.0,"dangling_components_rate":0.0,"intent_require_table":1.0,"intent_require_actions":1.0,"intent_require_sections":1.0,"intent_table_ok":0.0,"intent_actions_ok":0.0,"intent_sections_ok":0.0,"intent_expectation_pass":0.0,"intent_score":0.0},"gen":{"provider":"gemini","model":"gemini-2.5-pro","prompt_version":"genui_gen_v1","latency_ms":27372.692346572876,"input_tokens":1309,"output_tokens":2259,"cost_usd":null,"error":null},"created_at":"2026-02-11T19:59:19.097019Z"}
{"ui_id":"u_000401_01","response_id":"r_000401_01","query_id":"q_000401","intent":"Technical support","tags":["software troubleshooting","excel","data formatting"],"intent_bucket":"technical_support","genui_json":[{"version":"v0.9","createSurface":{"surfaceId":"surface_q_000401","catalogId":"https://genui.local/specification/v0_9/standard_catalog.json"}},{"version":"v0.9","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"surface_q_000401","components":[{"id":"root","component":"Column","children":["text_1"]},{"id":"text_1","component":"Text","text":"Summary:\nA common VLOOKUP error occurs when the lookup value and the first column of the source table have different data types (e.g., one is text, the other is a number), even if they appear identical. This response provides two methods to resolve this: a formula-based solution that converts the lookup value, and a formatting-based solution that converts the source data.\n\nAssumptions:\n*   The lookup value is in a cell (e.g., A2) and is currently formatted as text.\n*   The first column of your source table (where VLOOKUP searches) contains numbers, but the lookup value is not matching due to its text format.\n*   The VLOOKUP formula is otherwise correctly structured.\n\nSolution 1: Formula-Based Approach (Convert Lookup Value to Number)\nThis method modifies your VLOOKUP formula to convert the text lookup value into a number *before* performing the lookup, without altering your original data.\n\nExplanation:\nExcel's VLOOKUP requires the data types to match for an exact match. By converting the text lookup value to a number within the formula, you ensure compatibility with the numeric data in your source table.\n\nSteps:\n1.  Identify the cell containing your text-formatted lookup value (e.g., A2).\n2.  Modify your VLOOKUP formula to convert this text value to a number using a suitable function or operator.\n\nExample Formula:\nIf your original formula was =VLOOKUP(A2, B:C, 2, FALSE) and cell A2 contains a text value like \"123\", you can use one of these modified formulas:\n*   Using Double Unary Operator: =VLOOKUP(--A2, B:C, 2, FALSE)\n    (The double unary operator (--) is a concise way to convert text representations of numbers into actual numbers.)\n*   Using VALUE Function: =VLOOKUP(VALUE(A2), B:C, 2, FALSE)\n\nSolution 2: Formatting-Based Approach (Convert Source Data to Number)\nThis method involves permanently changing the data type of the relevant column in your source table from text to number.\n\nExplanation:\nIf the numbers in your source table's lookup column are stored as text (often indicated by a green triangle in the top-left corner of the cell), VLOOKUP will not find a match for a numeric lookup value. Converting these to actual numbers resolves the mismatch directly in your data.\n\nSteps:\n1.  Select the entire column or range in your source table that contains the numbers currently stored as text (e.g., column B).\n2.  Look for the \"Error Checking\" smart tag (a small yellow icon with an exclamation mark) that appears when you hover over the selection or click a cell with a green triangle.\n3.  Click the dropdown arrow next to the smart tag and select \"Convert to Number\".\n\nAlternative Methods to Convert Text to Number in Source Data:\n*   Text to Columns: Select the column, go to the \"Data\" tab on the Excel ribbon, click \"Text to Columns\", then simply click \"Finish\". This often forces the conversion to numbers.\n*   Paste Special (Multiply by 1):\n    1.  Enter the number 1 into an empty cell and copy it.\n    2.  Select the range of cells in your source table that contains the text-formatted numbers.\n    3.  Right-click on the selected range, choose \"Paste Special\", then select \"Multiply\" under \"Operation\", and click \"OK\".\n*   Add 0: Similar to \"Multiply by 1\", but copy a 0, then use \"Paste Special\" and \"Add\".\n\nComparison of Solutions:\nTable:\n| Solution Type       | Pros                                   | Cons                                      | Best Use Case                                |\n|---------------------|----------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|\n| Formula-Based       | Non-destructive to original source data| Requires modifying each VLOOKUP formula   | When source data cannot or should not be changed |\n| Formatting-Based    | Permanent fix for source data          | Modifies original data in the worksheet   | When source data needs to be clean numeric and is safe to alter |\n\nQuick Actions:\n[Button: Excel VLOOKUP Function Help] https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/vlookup-function-0398606e-09a8-4229-9e63-c7e6c40a5a67\n[Button: Convert Numbers Stored as Text to Numbers] https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/convert-numbers-stored-as-text-to-numbers-40a1b802-5509-480f-a0c0-6296b63a9404","variant":"body"}]}}],"assets":[],"toon":"[2]:\n  - version: v0.9\n    createSurface:\n      catalogId: \"https://genui.local/specification/v0_9/standard_catalog.json\"\n      surfaceId: surface_q_000401\n  - version: v0.9\n    updateComponents:\n      surfaceId: surface_q_000401\n      components[2]:\n        - component: Column\n          id: root\n          children[1]: text_1\n        - component: Text\n          id: text_1\n          text: \"Summary:\\nA common VLOOKUP error occurs when the lookup value and the first column of the source table have different data types (e.g., one is text, the other is a number), even if they appear identical. This response provides two methods to resolve this: a formula-based solution that converts the lookup value, and a formatting-based solution that converts the source data.\\n\\nAssumptions:\\n*   The lookup value is in a cell (e.g., A2) and is currently formatted as text.\\n*   The first column of your source table (where VLOOKUP searches) contains numbers, but the lookup value is not matching due to its text format.\\n*   The VLOOKUP formula is otherwise correctly structured.\\n\\nSolution 1: Formula-Based Approach (Convert Lookup Value to Number)\\nThis method modifies your VLOOKUP formula to convert the text lookup value into a number *before* performing the lookup, without altering your original data.\\n\\nExplanation:\\nExcel's VLOOKUP requires the data types to match for an exact match. By converting the text lookup value to a number within the formula, you ensure compatibility with the numeric data in your source table.\\n\\nSteps:\\n1.  Identify the cell containing your text-formatted lookup value (e.g., A2).\\n2.  Modify your VLOOKUP formula to convert this text value to a number using a suitable function or operator.\\n\\nExample Formula:\\nIf your original formula was =VLOOKUP(A2, B:C, 2, FALSE) and cell A2 contains a text value like \\\"123\\\", you can use one of these modified formulas:\\n*   Using Double Unary Operator: =VLOOKUP(--A2, B:C, 2, FALSE)\\n    (The double unary operator (--) is a concise way to convert text representations of numbers into actual numbers.)\\n*   Using VALUE Function: =VLOOKUP(VALUE(A2), B:C, 2, FALSE)\\n\\nSolution 2: Formatting-Based Approach (Convert Source Data to Number)\\nThis method involves permanently changing the data type of the relevant column in your source table from text to number.\\n\\nExplanation:\\nIf the numbers in your source table's lookup column are stored as text (often indicated by a green triangle in the top-left corner of the cell), VLOOKUP will not find a match for a numeric lookup value. Converting these to actual numbers resolves the mismatch directly in your data.\\n\\nSteps:\\n1.  Select the entire column or range in your source table that contains the numbers currently stored as text (e.g., column B).\\n2.  Look for the \\\"Error Checking\\\" smart tag (a small yellow icon with an exclamation mark) that appears when you hover over the selection or click a cell with a green triangle.\\n3.  Click the dropdown arrow next to the smart tag and select \\\"Convert to Number\\\".\\n\\nAlternative Methods to Convert Text to Number in Source Data:\\n*   Text to Columns: Select the column, go to the \\\"Data\\\" tab on the Excel ribbon, click \\\"Text to Columns\\\", then simply click \\\"Finish\\\". This often forces the conversion to numbers.\\n*   Paste Special (Multiply by 1):\\n    1.  Enter the number 1 into an empty cell and copy it.\\n    2.  Select the range of cells in your source table that contains the text-formatted numbers.\\n    3.  Right-click on the selected range, choose \\\"Paste Special\\\", then select \\\"Multiply\\\" under \\\"Operation\\\", and click \\\"OK\\\".\\n*   Add 0: Similar to \\\"Multiply by 1\\\", but copy a 0, then use \\\"Paste Special\\\" and \\\"Add\\\".\\n\\nComparison of Solutions:\\nTable:\\n| Solution Type       | Pros                                   | Cons                                      | Best Use Case                                |\\n|---------------------|----------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|\\n| Formula-Based       | Non-destructive to original source data| Requires modifying each VLOOKUP formula   | When source data cannot or should not be changed |\\n| Formatting-Based    | Permanent fix for source data          | Modifies original data in the worksheet   | When source data needs to be clean numeric and is safe to alter |\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Excel VLOOKUP Function Help] https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/vlookup-function-0398606e-09a8-4229-9e63-c7e6c40a5a67\\n[Button: Convert Numbers Stored as Text to Numbers] https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/convert-numbers-stored-as-text-to-numbers-40a1b802-5509-480f-a0c0-6296b63a9404\"\n          variant: body","validation":{"json_parse_ok":true,"schema_valid_strict":true,"schema_valid_lenient":true,"toon_roundtrip_ok":true,"errors":["fallback_generated"],"repair_attempts":0,"repair_needed":false},"metr