 Liner socks (optional, helps prevent blisters).\n- Gloves (2 pairs):\n  - Thin liner gloves.\n  - Warm, waterproof outer gloves or mittens.\n- Headwear (2-3 items):\n  - Warm wool or fleece beanie/hat.\n  - Sun hat or cap.\n  - Neck gaiter or buff.\n- Sleepwear:\n  - Can use a clean set of base layers.\n\nGear:\n- Backpacks:\n  - Large duffel bag (80-100L) for porter (often provided by trekking agency, confirm).\n  - Daypack (25-35L) for personal daily essentials (water, snacks, camera, layers).\n- Sleeping Bag:\n  - Rated to at least -10°C (comfort rating), possibly -15°C to -20°C for colder sleepers.\n- Footwear:\n  - Sturdy, waterproof, broken-in trekking boots (mid to high-ankle support).\n  - Camp shoes, sandals, or crocs for evenings in teahouses.\n- Trekking Poles:\n  - Highly recommended for stability and reducing knee strain.\n- Water Storage:\n  - 2 x 1L Nalgene bottles (can be filled with hot water for warmth at night).\n  - Hydration reservoir (2-3L, ensure tube doesn't freeze at high altitude).\n- Headlamp:\n  - With fresh batteries and spare set.\n- Eyewear:\n  - High-quality sunglasses with UV protection (Category 3 or 4).\n- Electronics:\n  - Power bank/portable charger.\n  - Camera, extra batteries, memory cards.\n  - Phone and charger.\n  - Universal travel adapter (Nepal uses Type C, D, and M sockets).\n- Miscellaneous:\n  - Quick-dry towel.\n  - Small padlock for duffel bag.\n  - Dry bags or plastic bags for organizing clothes and electronics.\n  - Book, journal, pen.\n  - Trekking map (optional, often provided by guide).\n  - Small thermos (optional, for hot drinks).\n  - Snacks (energy bars, dried fruit, nuts, chocolate - personal preference).\n  - Reusable shopping bag (for souvenirs).\n\nHygiene & Personal Care:\n- Toiletries:\n  - Toothbrush, toothpaste.\n  - Biodegradable soap/shampoo (small travel sizes).\n  - Deodorant.\n  - Small mirror (optional).\n- Hand Sanitizer:\n  - Essential for hygiene on the trail.\n- Wet Wipes:\n  - For sponge