{"ui_id":"u_000321_01","response_id":"r_000321_01","query_id":"q_000321","intent":"Recipe","tags":["dinner","italian","classic"],"intent_bucket":"recipe","genui_json":[{"version":"v0.9","createSurface":{"surfaceId":"surface_q_000321","catalogId":"https://genui.local/specification/v0_9/standard_catalog.json"}},{"version":"v0.9","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"surface_q_000321","components":[{"id":"root","component":"Column","children":["text_1"]},{"id":"text_1","component":"Text","text":"Traditional Italian Bolognese Sauce (Serves 4)\n\nSummary:\nThis recipe provides a traditional, rich, and deeply flavorful Bolognese sauce, perfect for serving with fresh pasta. The key to its authentic taste is a slow, patient simmer over three hours, allowing the flavors to meld beautifully. This process creates a thick, luxurious sauce that is a staple of Italian cuisine.\n\nIngredients:\n-   Olive Oil: 30 ml (2 tablespoons)\n-   Pancetta, diced: 100 grams\n-   Yellow Onion, finely chopped: 150 grams (about 1 medium)\n-   Carrot, finely chopped: 100 grams (about 1 medium)\n-   Celery Stalk, finely chopped: 100 grams (about 1-2 stalks)\n-   Ground Beef (80/20 blend recommended): 300 grams\n-   Ground Pork: 200 grams\n-   Whole Milk: 200 ml\n-   Dry White Wine: 200 ml\n-   Canned Crushed Tomatoes (or tomato passata): 400 grams\n-   Tomato Paste: 30 grams (2 tablespoons)\n-   Beef Broth (or water): 250 ml\n-   Salt: 5 grams (1 teaspoon), plus more to taste\n-   Freshly Ground Black Pepper: 2 grams (1/2 teaspoon), plus more to taste\n-   Freshly Grated Nutmeg: 1 gram (1/4 teaspoon)\n\nInstructions:\n1.  Prepare the Soffritto: Heat the olive oil in a large, heavy-bottomed pot or Dutch oven over medium heat. Add the diced pancetta and cook until crispy and its fat has rendered, about 5-7 minutes. Remove the crispy pancetta with a slotted spoon and set aside, leaving the rendered fat in the pot.\n2.  Sauté Vegetables: Add the finely chopped onion, carrot, and celery (the \"soffritto\") to the pot. Cook over medium-low heat, stirring occasionally, until the vegetables are very soft and translucent, about 10-15 minutes. Do not let them brown.\n3.  Brown the Meats: Increase the heat to medium. Add the ground beef and ground pork to the pot. Break up the meat with a spoon and cook until it is fully browned and no pink remains, about 8-10 minutes. Drain off any excess fat if necessary.\n4.  Deglaze with Milk and Wine: Pour in the whole milk and bring to a gentle simmer. Cook, stirring occasionally, until the milk has almost completely evaporated and been absorbed by the meat, about 10-15 minutes. This step adds richness and tenderizes the meat. Next, add the dry white wine. Bring to a simmer and cook until the wine has almost completely evaporated, about 5-7 minutes.\n5.  Add Tomatoes and Broth: Stir in the crushed tomatoes, tomato paste, beef broth, salt, pepper, and nutmeg. Return the crispy pancetta to the pot. Bring the sauce to a gentle simmer.\n6.  Slow Simmer: Once simmering, reduce the heat to very low, cover the pot partially (leaving a small gap for steam to escape), and let the sauce gently simmer for at least 3 hours. Stir the sauce every 30-45 minutes to prevent sticking and ensure even cooking. If the sauce becomes too thick during simmering, add a splash more beef broth or water. The goal is a thick, rich sauce.\n7.  Finish and Serve: After 3 hours, the sauce should be deeply colored, thick, and incredibly flavorful. Taste and adjust seasoning (salt, pepper, or nutmeg) if needed. Serve hot with your favorite pasta, traditionally tagliatelle, and a generous grating of Parmigiano-Reggiano cheese.\n\nImages:\n-   Bolognese Sauce Simmering: https://example.com/bolognese_simmering.jpg\n-   Soffritto Ingredients: https://example.com/soffritto_ingredients.jpg\n\nQuick Actions:\n[Button: Find Pasta Pairings] https://example.com/pasta-pairings\n[Button: Shop Bolognese Ingredients Online] https://example.com/shop-bolognese-ingredients","variant":"body"}]}}],"assets":[],"toon":"[2]:\n  - version: v0.9\n    createSurface:\n      catalogId: \"https://genui.local/specification/v0_9/standard_catalog.json\"\n      surfaceId: surface_q_000321\n  - version: v0.9\n    updateComponents:\n      surfaceId: surface_q_000321\n      components[2]:\n        - component: Column\n          id: root\n          children[1]: text_1\n        - component: Text\n          id: text_1\n          text: \"Traditional Italian Bolognese Sauce (Serves 4)\\n\\nSummary:\\nThis recipe provides a traditional, rich, and deeply flavorful Bolognese sauce, perfect for serving with fresh pasta. The key to its authentic taste is a slow, patient simmer over three hours, allowing the flavors to meld beautifully. This process creates a thick, luxurious sauce that is a staple of Italian cuisine.\\n\\nIngredients:\\n-   Olive Oil: 30 ml (2 tablespoons)\\n-   Pancetta, diced: 100 grams\\n-   Yellow Onion, finely chopped: 150 grams (about 1 medium)\\n-   Carrot, finely chopped: 100 grams (about 1 medium)\\n-   Celery Stalk, finely chopped: 100 grams (about 1-2 stalks)\\n-   Ground Beef (80/20 blend recommended): 300 grams\\n-   Ground Pork: 200 grams\\n-   Whole Milk: 200 ml\\n-   Dry White Wine: 200 ml\\n-   Canned Crushed Tomatoes (or tomato passata): 400 grams\\n-   Tomato Paste: 30 grams (2 tablespoons)\\n-   Beef Broth (or water): 250 ml\\n-   Salt: 5 grams (1 teaspoon), plus more to taste\\n-   Freshly Ground Black Pepper: 2 grams (1/2 teaspoon), plus more to taste\\n-   Freshly Grated Nutmeg: 1 gram (1/4 teaspoon)\\n\\nInstructions:\\n1.  Prepare the Soffritto: Heat the olive oil in a large, heavy-bottomed pot or Dutch oven over medium heat. Add the diced pancetta and cook until crispy and its fat has rendered, about 5-7 minutes. Remove the crispy pancetta with a slotted spoon and set aside, leaving the rendered fat in the pot.\\n2.  Sauté Vegetables: Add the finely chopped onion, carrot, and celery (the \\\"soffritto\\\") to the pot. Cook over medium-low heat, stirring occasionally, until the vegetables are very soft and translucent, about 10-15 minutes. Do not let them brown.\\n3.  Brown the Meats: Increase the heat to medium. Add the ground beef and ground pork to the pot. Break up the meat with a spoon and cook until it is fully browned and no pink remains, about 8-10 minutes. Drain off any excess fat if necessary.\\n4.  Deglaze with Milk and Wine: Pour in the whole milk and bring to a gentle simmer. Cook, stirring occasionally, until the milk has almost completely evaporated and been absorbed by the meat, about 10-15 minutes. This step adds richness and tenderizes the meat. Next, add the dry white wine. Bring to a simmer and cook until the wine has almost completely evaporated, about 5-7 minutes.\\n5.  Add Tomatoes and Broth: Stir in the crushed tomatoes, tomato paste, beef broth, salt, pepper, and nutmeg. Return the crispy pancetta to the pot. Bring the sauce to a gentle simmer.\\n6.  Slow Simmer: Once simmering, reduce the heat to very low, cover the pot partially (leaving a small gap for steam to escape), and let the sauce gently simmer for at least 3 hours. Stir the sauce every 30-45 minutes to prevent sticking and ensure even cooking. If the sauce becomes too thick during simmering, add a splash more beef broth or water. The goal is a thick, rich sauce.\\n7.  Finish and Serve: After 3 hours, the sauce should be deeply colored, thick, and incredibly flavorful. Taste and adjust seasoning (salt, pepper, or nutmeg) if needed. Serve hot with your favorite pasta, traditionally tagliatelle, and a generous grating of Parmigiano-Reggiano cheese.\\n\\nImages:\\n-   Bolognese Sauce Simmering: https://example.com/bolognese_simmering.jpg\\n-   Soffritto Ingredients: https://example.com/soffritto_ingredients.jpg\\n\\nQuick Actions:\\n[Button: Find Pasta Pairings] https://example.com/pasta-pairings\\n[Button: Shop Bolognese Ingredients Online] https://example.com/shop-bolognese-ingredients\"\n          variant: bo